Subtask 4.3: Create Storage Backend Comparison
Create a comparison document:

# Create comparison file
cat > ~/vault-lab/storage-backend-comparison.md << 'EOF'
# Vault Storage Backend Comparison

## File Storage Backend

### Advantages:
- Simple setup and configuration
- No external dependencies
- Good for development and testing
- Fast local access
- Easy backup (just copy files)

### Disadvantages:
- No high availability
- Single point of failure
- Not suitable for production clusters
- Limited scalability
- No built-in replication

### Use Cases:
- Development environments
- Testing and proof of concepts
- Single-node deployments
- Local development

## Consul Storage Backend

### Advantages:
- High availability and clustering support
- Built-in replication and consistency
- Service discovery integration
- Mature and battle-tested
- Supports multi-datacenter deployments

### Disadvantages:
- Additional complexity
- Requires Consul cluster management
- Network dependency
- Slightly higher latency than file storage

### Use Cases:
- Production environments
- High availability requirements
- Multi-node Vault clusters
- Integration with Consul service mesh

## Performance Observations:
- File storage: Faster for single-node operations
- Consul storage: Better for distributed operations
- Network latency affects Consul performance
- File storage has lower resource overhead
EOF

# Display comparison
cat ~/vault-lab/storage-backend-comparison.md
Task 5: Advanced Configuration and Security
Subtask 5.1: Configure Storage Backend Security
Enhance Consul storage security:

# Stop current Vault instance
pkill vault
sleep 3

# Create secure Consul configuration
cat > ~/vault-lab/config/consul-secure.hcl << 'EOF'
datacenter = "dc1"
data_dir = "/opt/consul"
log_level = "INFO"
server = true
bootstrap_expect = 1
bind_addr = "127.0.0.1"
client_addr = "127.0.0.1"
retry_join = ["127.0.0.1"]

# Enable ACLs
acl = {
  enabled = true
  default_policy = "deny"
  enable_token_persistence = true
}

ui_config {
  enabled = true
}

connect {
  enabled = true
}

ports {
  grpc = 8502
}
EOF

# Restart Consul with ACLs
sudo pkill consul
sleep 3
sudo -u consul consul agent -config-file=~/vault-lab/config/consul-secure.hcl > ~/vault-lab/consul-secure.log 2>&1 &
sleep 10

# Bootstrap ACL system
consul acl bootstrap > ~/vault-lab/consul-bootstrap.txt
CONSUL_TOKEN=$(grep 'SecretID:' ~/vault-lab/consul-bootstrap.txt | awk '{print $2}')

echo "Consul Bootstrap Token: $CONSUL_TOKEN"
Subtask 5.2: Create Vault-Specific Consul Policy
Create a dedicated policy for Vault:

# Set Consul token
export CONSUL_HTTP_TOKEN=$CONSUL_TOKEN

# Create Vault policy
cat > ~/vault-lab/vault-consul-policy.hcl << 'EOF'
key_prefix "vault/" {
  policy = "write"
}

node_prefix "" {
  policy = "write"
}

service "vault" {
  policy = "write"
}

agent_prefix "" {
  policy = "write"
}

session_prefix "" {
  policy = "write"
}
EOF

# Apply the policy
consul acl policy create -name "vault-policy" -description "Policy for Vault" -rules @~/vault-lab/vault-consul-policy.hcl

# Create token for Vault
consul acl token create -description "Vault Token" -policy-name "vault-policy" > ~/vault-lab/vault-consul-token.txt
VAULT_CONSUL_TOKEN=$(grep 'SecretID:' ~/vault-lab/vault-consul-token.txt | awk '{print $2}')

echo "Vault Consul Token: $VAULT_CONSUL_TOKEN"
Subtask 5.3: Configure Vault with Secure Consul Backend
Update Vault configuration to use the secure token:

# Create secure Vault configuration
cat > ~/vault-lab/config/vault-consul-secure.hcl << 'EOF'
storage "consul" {
  address = "127.0.0.1:8500"
  path    = "vault/"
  token   = "VAULT_CONSUL_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER"
}

